#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
FAMILY 6 : REACTIVE REGIME ADAPTATION  (f6_reactive-adapt)
==========================================================

THESIS (from scout): regime CANNOT be predicted at the open (AUC == placebo).
So do not predict it.  Let the session declare itself:  run a continuously
re-evaluated state machine on realised directional efficiency (ER) and route a
*fixed trigger set* into either a TREND (continuation) or MR (fade) playbook.

EXPERIMENTAL DESIGN -- deliberately symmetric so the ONLY thing being tested is
the adaptation:

    trigger set  T  = bars where some neutral event fires
                      (A: new K2-bar closing extreme,  B: |c-EMA| >= z*ATR)
    TREND playbook  = take trigger in the CONTINUATION direction
    MR    playbook  = take the SAME trigger in the FADE direction

    REACTIVE      : state machine picks direction  (TREND / MR / NEUTRAL=skip)
    ALWAYS-TREND  : every trigger, continuation direction
    ALWAYS-MR     : every trigger, fade direction
    RANDOM-SWITCH : state series circularly ROTATED inside each day-cycle
                    -> identical switch frequency, identical state occupancy,
                       identical run-length distribution, alignment destroyed.

Because trend/MR are literally the same bars with opposite sign, this isolates
"does ER tell you which way to take the trigger?" with no trade-count confound.

Everything is causal.  See lookahead_audit() -- run before any result is
believed.
"""

import os, sys, json, math, warnings
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from numba import njit

warnings.filterwarnings("ignore")
np.set_printoptions(suppress=True, precision=4)
DIR = "/Users/kaveh/qt_cascade"
OUT = os.path.join(DIR, "f6_reactive_adapt_out")   # unique -- f6_out is taken by another agent
